3) Location: Section 2, proof of Lemma 2.4(i).
Severity: MINOR.

The displayed approximation for alpha proves alpha -> 1 and bounds |1-alpha| log B. By itself it does not establish alpha <= 1, which the upper bound c^(alpha-1) <= 1 uses.

Fix: State the defining saddlepoint equation
sum_{p<=B} log p/(p^alpha-1) = log x.
At alpha=1, the left side is O(log B), whereas log x is a fixed positive multiple of log N. Monotonicity therefore gives alpha<1 throughout the range used here. The remainder of the proof follows.

The quoted Hildebrand–Tenenbaum ratio theorem has the appropriate range. [Hildebrand–Tenenbaum, Theorem 3](https://tenenb.perso.math.cnrs.fr/PPP/Psi%2B.pdf)

4) Location: Section 3, generalised base-expansion lemma.
Severity: MINOR.

“The integer” satisfying |a_i| <= g_1/2 and the specified congruence is not unique when g_1 is even and the residue is g_1/2. A deterministic field law requires a tie rule.

Fix: Use the half-open interval -g_1/2 < a_i <= g_1/2. All stated bounds remain valid.

5) Location: Section 3, numerical example immediately following the size-relations lemma.
Severity: MINOR.

At d=9, k=13, the algebraic exponent is incorrectly printed as 59/156 + eta_0. It is
17/156 + 6/156 + 27/156 + eta_0
= 50/156 + eta_0
= 25/78 + eta_0.

Together with the rational exponent 14/156, this gives theta=64/156+eta_0=16/39+eta_0, as required elsewhere.

Fix: Replace 59/156 by 50/156.

6) Location: Section 8, conclusion of the good-modulus lemma.
Severity: MINOR.

The inequality V/H <= N^(-nu-eta_0) drops the positive L factor in V. It is generally false when h=2nu+eta_0.

Fix: Write
V/H = N^(nu-h+o(1)) <= N^(-nu-eta_0+o(1)),
or use the eventual bound N^(-nu-eta_0/2). Either is more than sufficient to make the total-variation error negligible.

7) Location: Section 9, Proposition “Theorem R”, covering argument.
Severity: MINOR.

The asserted inequality w_0=M/V >= X_r/(2S^2) has the wrong constant. Since X_r=2 M V_max/Y, it would require V V_max <= Y S^2, which need not hold.

Fix: Use
w_0 >= X_r/(4S^2),
which follows from V V_max <= V_max^2 <= 2YS^2. Divisor switching only needs a fixed L-power comparison, so the proposition is unaffected. This proposition is not used in the main proof.

8) Location: Section 10, proof of the residual square-class group bound.
Severity: MINOR.

The asserted coefficient bound ||f||_infinity <= M(H+1) is false. An interior coefficient contains g_1 c_i - g_0 c_{i-1}, whose magnitude can approach 2MH.

Fix: Use ||f||_infinity <= C_d M(H+1), or, for sufficiently large N, a suitable explicit constant such as 3. Since mu+h<1, the corrected bound is still eventually below N and still gives log|D|, log|disc g|=O_d(log N).

The source notes retain a constant in this bound; its disappearance is a distillation error.

9) Location: Section 10, definition of t_chi.
Severity: MINOR.

The algorithm needs a computable upper bound for |H|. The displayed bound 2^(d omega(D)+d) h_K involves quantities that the algorithm has not computed. The asymptotic statement log|H|=O_d(log N) alone is not an executable specification.

Fix: Give an explicit bound requiring neither factorisation of D nor computation of h_K. For example, with X=ceil(sqrt(|disc g|)),
H_bd = 2^d |D|^d X(1+log X)^(d-1)
suffices. Use this bound in t_chi.

10) Location: Section 10, Proposition 10.15, kernel-sampling complexity.
Severity: MINOR.

The precise operation count attributed to Kaltofen–Saunders omits logarithmic factors. Their Toeplitz preconditioning and rank computation do not directly give the stated O(M_rows * entries + M_rows^2) field-operation bound.

Fix: State a soft-O bound, or include the logarithmic factors explicitly. They are absorbed by the final M_rows^2 (log N)^O(1) bound, so the factoring exponent does not change. The paper’s separate argument for obtaining an exactly uniform binary kernel vector is valid. [Kaltofen–Saunders, Theorems 3–4](https://kaltofen.math.ncsu.edu/bibliography/91/KaSa91.pdf)

11) Location: Section 7, field-selection proposition, proof of part (ii).
Severity: MINOR.

The proof divides by b without separating the possibility b=0. Positive p does not imply positive b: all successful trials could be factor exits.

Fix: Handle b=0 first. Selection then terminates with a factor in expected cost at most C_trial/p. For b>0, the posterior law and harmonic-mean calculation are correct.

Also use a deterministic upper bound for M_rows when writing an unconditional expected-cost inequality, since the actual column count depends on the selected field.

12) Location: Sections 3 and 13, small-input dovetailing.
Severity: MINOR.

Dovetailing repairs missing asymptotic success estimates, but it does not automatically define invalid arithmetic in the SPLIT branch. For small inputs one can have M=1, g_1=1, an empty J, or unavailable subroutine prerequisites.

Fix: Add explicit guards that make the SPLIT branch return FAIL or yield whenever its structural prerequisites fail. In particular, never invoke the base-expansion lemma with g_1<2 or sample from an empty set. Continue the trial-division branch independently. These guards eventually disappear and have no asymptotic cost.

13) Location: Section 13, uniformity over recursive inputs.
Severity: MINOR.

The assertion that replacing an unspecified o(1) by a non-increasing envelope “costs only a polylogarithmic factor” is not justified as stated. Monotonicity of that envelope does not itself make the resulting running-time bound monotone.

Fix: Use the standard epsilon argument. For every epsilon>0, all sufficiently large recursive inputs m have expected cost at most L_m[1/2,c+epsilon] <= L_N[1/2,c+epsilon]. The finitely many smaller inputs have bounded cost through trial division. Summing over O(log N) recursive calls proves the desired bound.

14) Location: Appendix A, Fact “Lee–Venkatesan”.
Severity: MINOR.

The fact is stated over the full published y range, although the manuscript’s own final remark acknowledges that its absorption of logarithmic losses is justified only in a narrower regime. The broad quantified statement is therefore insufficiently justified by the argument presented.

Fix: State the imported character-majorant estimate only in the regime actually needed:
x=N^(xi+o(1)), y=B=L_N[1/2,a], and Q'=N^(q'+o(1)) with fixed positive exponent margins, taking omega_0=log x.

In that regime, u/log^2 u >> log log x, and the logarithmic losses are absorbed. The published proof explicitly introduces and bounds the character majorant W, so using its interior is legitimate; the TODO is not, by itself, a missing hypothesis. [Lee–Venkatesan, equation (8.2) and proof of Lemma 8.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

15) Location: Section 10, definition of quadratic characters on H.
Severity: PRESENTATION.

The expression gamma times a power of the prime ideal is not a field element whose Legendre symbol can be taken.

Fix: Choose a local uniformiser pi at the sampled prime and use gamma*pi^(-ord gamma). Since ord gamma is even, changing pi changes the residue by a square. For the actual atoms, the sampled primes already have valuation zero, so their displayed evaluation formula needs no modification.

16) Location: Section 10, square-root lemma, inverse-Vandermonde coefficient bound.
Severity: PRESENTATION.

The bound on the numerator coefficients is correct, but its justification is omitted. Bounding each root separately by Mh would give a much weaker bound.

Fix: Invoke the Mahler-measure inequality. For
g(y)/(y-beta_k)=product_{j!=k}(y-beta_j),
each coefficient is bounded by
2^(d-1) product_j max(1,|beta_j|)
=2^(d-1) M(g)
<=2^(d-1)||g||_2.
This proves the claimed numerator bound and hence A_sq.

17) Location: Introduction, main statement and “what is and is not claimed”.
Severity: PRESENTATION.

Three formulations need correction.

First, write “expected time at most L_N[...]”. Literal asymptotic equality is false for the stated algorithm on prime inputs, which stop after polynomial-time primality testing.

Second, “the best single algorithm we analyse” at d=9 is not c approximately 0.9972: the paper also analyses every larger fixed k, including the smaller constants in its own table. Say “the example d=9, k=13”.

Third, Section 3 requires rational eta_0, whereas the introductory admissibility definition does not. Make the convention consistent. Restricting eta_0 to positive rationals leaves the corollary unchanged.

18) Location: Table 1, “need eta_0<” column.
Severity: PRESENTATION.

The decimals are correctly rounded, but they must not be read as exact sufficient cutoffs. In particular,
sqrt(2)-1-16/39 = 0.003957152116...
is smaller than 0.00396.

Fix: Label the column as an approximate threshold, or print the exact expression sqrt(2)-1-theta_min(d,k).

19) Location: Introduction, prior-art paragraph; Section 15, recursive numerical example.
Severity: PRESENTATION.

The phrase excluding earlier rigorous c<1 results “conditional or unconditional” is too broad and conflicts with the discussion of conditional NFS results. The relevant novelty claim is an unconditional classical bound for every integer. The conditional factoring conclusion in Lee–Venkatesan has a restricted input class. [Lee–Venkatesan, Theorem 2.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

Also, the speculative recursive constant approximately 0.851 at d=9 uses the infimum theta=0.3, not the displayed fixed parameters d=9,k=13.

Fix: Narrow the novelty wording and label 0.851 as an infimum in the unproved recursive model. I found no earlier unconditional classical c<1 bound for every integer in the sources checked.

20) Location: Bibliography.
Severity: PRESENTATION.

The Joux–Lercier title omits its subtitle, “A comparison with the Gaussian integer method”. The Bernstein draft entries should identify the actual drafts rather than merely linking to cr.yp.to.

Fix: Restore the complete Joux–Lercier title and give precise draft links and version dates. Bernstein’s own bibliography identifies the relevant versions as 2002-09-23 and 2004-05-10. [Joux–Lercier paper](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=c0f2bad7857b21d1c5abd54b28ca4e87b280e336&repid=rep1&type=pdf), [Bernstein’s bibliography](https://cr.yp.to/factorization.html)

The central bibliography data for LP, LV, Harper, Hildebrand–Tenenbaum, Tenenbaum, Montgomery–Vaughan, Lagarias–Odlyzko and Stark agree with the records checked.


Section 1: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

The fixed-parameter quantifiers and the distinction between an infimum and an attained o(1) constant are substantively correct. Choosing d, then k, then sufficiently small positive rational eta_0 gives the stated “for every c>1/sqrt(2)” corollary.

