Section 2: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

CEP is used in its appropriate large-u range. The actual arguments have x=N^(positive constant+o(1)) and B=L^a, comfortably inside that range.

The Hildebrand–Tenenbaum ratio estimate is appropriate. Lemma 2.4 needs the saddlepoint observation in issue 3; its remaining deductions are valid. The fixed-Z interval estimate does not require a shrinking relative interval.

Tenenbaum’s theorem applies first to the B-smooth part of the modulus. Removing larger prime factors changes the Euler factor by 1+O(omega(q)/B), giving the stated extension. For the moduli used here, its relative error is o(1). [Tenenbaum’s theorem and first remark](https://tenenb.perso.math.cnrs.fr/PPP/Psi_q(x,y).pdf)

The LP input is correctly a complete-factorisation expected-time bound. AKS certification does not change its L exponent. [Lenstra–Pomerance](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf)

The additive large sieve and interval Brun–Titchmarsh statements have the required constants and ranges. [Montgomery–Vaughan, Theorems 1–2](https://personal.science.psu.edu/rcv4/personal/Publications/large_sieve.pdf)


Section 3: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

The parameter optimisation is correct. At zero slack,
theta(mu)=max(2mu+(d+1)nu, 3mu+nu, 1+(2-d)mu+dnu),
and the stated intersections give the two branches.

The size floors provide all three essential positive margins:

mu-nu >= eta_0;
h-2nu >= eta_0;
h-mu+(d-2)nu >= eta_0.

The constants in the value-size lemma are sufficient. In particular, the large constant coefficient is suppressed by N^(-eta_0/2), and C'_d accommodates both that term and Gz.

Homogeneity and the forced-residue congruence are correct. Coprimality genuinely follows from prime b>B, smooth n, ell not dividing N, and w>|G(P)|; it is not being assumed for arbitrary candidates.

The regularity lemma is correct. In the irregular case, noncentral lattice lines have distance greater than 2V from the origin. The only primitive central-line points are too short to satisfy b>V/2. In the regular case the displayed estimate gives
8S^2+(512/3)S^2 < 200S^2.

The operational search matches the counted relations. Its decisions do not inspect the hidden square root. Fix the base-expansion tie, numerical typo and small-input guards identified above.


Section 4: CORRECT.

The dependency order is appropriate. In particular, moments are taken under the raw product law, rejection is charged afterwards through bounded delta_A, and field selection comes last.

The distinction between L^(-o(1)) and a polylogarithmic lower bound for gamma is important and correctly maintained.


Section 5: CORRECT.

Theorem S has the correct h_2 factor. Character positivity supplies the first moment; proportionality and the gcd sum supply the second.

The shortest-vector argument is valid: both vectors have norm at most sqrt(2)Y', their determinant is a multiple of N, and its absolute value is less than N because 2Y'^2<N. Primitivity in the lattice then gives an integer multiple.

For S', the binomial overcount is legitimate, the sqrt(2) estimate follows from n>=4k^2, and summing binom(k,i)^2 gives the stated c_k.

The bound 2^omega(N)=L^o(1) after trial division is also correct.


Section 6: CORRECT.

The measurable random finite index set is sufficient: conditional on all squares and independent coins, its selected unit fibres retain independent uniform signs. No independence of accepted squares is required.

The exact success probability remains valid for odd nonsquarefree inputs, because roots of unity modulo each odd prime power are exactly ±1.

One sentence should be read more carefully: the numerical value gcd(r,N) is not always a function of r^2 modulo a nonsquarefree N. Its unit/nonunit status is. Since nonunit roots cause immediate factor exits, the continuing transcript still has the required invariance. State this distinction explicitly.


Section 7: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

Theorem K correctly uses closure under taking divisors: the shortest split can lose shared primes, so “at most k distinct primes” is essential and sufficient.

The N versus N-1 denominator is conservative, not an error.

After the b=0 case is separated, field selection is correct. The selected-field law is proportional to varsigma_A, and
E[1/varsigma_A*] <= 1/b
is the appropriate identity.

Wald’s argument does not require independence between a trial’s cost and its outcome. Independence of fresh trial blocks, or the stated conditional cost bound together with a stopping-time sum, suffices.

Factor exits are incorporated as successful outcomes. I find no illicit conditioning on a favourable field or on acceptance.


Section 8: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

Lemma 8.1 survives the detailed audit.

The adjacent kernel vectors have maximal minors with gcd 1, so they generate the full saturated kernel. This is stronger than merely spanning its rational vector space.

The representation argument for every integer |m|<=||v||_2 is valid. The orthogonal real representative has infinity norm at most 1; reduction into the fundamental cell adds at most 2V', giving 2V'+1.

The smoothing construction produces independent contiguous integer uniforms. The translation bound gives precisely 3d^2 V'/(2H+1). Symmetry is preserved by convolution; log-concavity gives unimodality, and therefore nested symmetric interval level sets. The layer-cake decomposition is valid. The maximum-mass bound follows from the factor having l_i=V'^(d-1).

The good-modulus lemma correctly discards small layers and uses large layers whose lengths dominate both their centre displacement and the base-polynomial shift. The common grid is independent of all residues. Its lower endpoint remains N^(xi+o(1)), and comparisons by L^O(1)=B^O(1) lose only polylogarithms.

The fibre lemma is correct. Its Möbius error 2^omega(ell|n|)=N^o(1) is compared with the positive power M/V, not silently treated as L^o(1).

For the first moment, the relevant powers are:
(V^2/log V) * (1/M^2) * (M^2 rho_r/(ell log log N)) * rho_F/polylog,
which gives S^2 rho_r rho_F/polylog.

After correcting beta_ell, the bad mass has the same geometric scale multiplied by epsilon_N times a fixed polylogarithm. Thus the claimed first moment follows.


Section 9: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

The pair lemma is correct, including the bound of 12 partners for fixed P and q.

Kernel transfer is correct. After sending P to (1,0), the gcd of the evaluation minors is |Delta|, not a higher power of Delta. The kernel consists exactly of multiples of the two primitive linear factors. The determinant calculation, fundamental-cell bound and wedge minor give the claimed probability bound.

Brun–Titchmarsh spreading is valid. The proof actually provides 12/eta_0; the stated 24/eta_0 is safe.

Divisor switching is valid for arbitrary residues, including nonreduced ones. The extracted smooth divisor lies in (D/B,D], and D>=XB^(-2C_0). The reciprocal-divisor sum therefore costs only a polylogarithm.

The L^2 family proof is internally consistent. Its relative error terms have exponents:

Class B: N^(-h-(d+1)nu) L^O(1).

Class C: N^(-2nu) L^O(1), and
N^((5-d)nu-h) L^O(1).

The last exponent is at most -eta_0 when d=3 and is more negative for larger d. These are genuine power savings.

The class-C use of two different multiplicity bounds is legitimate: a common upper bound by their minimum permits assigning the first bound to the length term and the second to the denominator-squared term.

The second-moment order of operations is correct. AM–GM precedes enlargement; enlargement removes the w dependence except through one residue; w averaging then removes that residue before the rational fibre estimate. No joint distribution of the two forced residues is needed.

The partner estimate retains the second rho_r factor. Substitution cancels M and H exactly and leaves V^4/Y^2 asymptotic to S^4. The q sum contributes only a polylogarithm.

Paley–Zygmund is applied correctly even when the mean tends to zero. Its displayed denominator P^3+P/m is algebraically correct.

The unused rational-pair proposition needs only the constant correction in issue 7.


Section 10: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

Lemma IR’s affine coefficient distributions and CRT argument are valid. The prescribed-norm count gives the required irreducible fraction, and the numerical exponent 13d has sufficient slack.

The rejection estimates are valid once their raw-law events are defined correctly. Content w costs at most 1/W+1/(2H+1); the derivative event costs at most 1/(2H+1).

The signed row identity is correct, including negative rational values, denominator exponents and repeated cached atoms. The single parity column handles both g_1^E and f_d^(-E).

Lemma C correctly excludes primes dividing f_d disc(f), assigns the remaining valuations to degree-one primes, and uses norm valuations appropriately.

The obstruction-group bound is correct after correcting the coefficient height. It includes units, class-group 2-torsion and primes above D.

The relative quadratic discriminant bound is sufficient, including dyadic primes.

The quoted Lagarias–Odlyzko threshold is the appropriate unconditional one. It applies to L_q/K, which is Galois of degree two; normality over Q is unnecessary. [Lagarias–Odlyzko, Theorem 1.3](https://aareyanmanzoor.github.io/assets/articles/lagarias-odlyzko.pdf)

The exceptional-class argument works: odd d excludes quadratic subfields of K and biquadratic subfields of L_q; Stark descends a sufficiently close zero to a quadratic subfield; Landau allows at most one such quadratic field. Kummer injectivity then gives |E|<=1.

The character sampler is uniform on degree-one prime ideals. The LO main term, exceptional-zero decay and removal of higher-degree primes leave ample room for 1/(5d).

SQ is complete as well as sound. The codifferent inclusion puts an existing square root in Z[beta]; the local sign enumeration covers it up to global sign; centred reconstruction recovers it at the stated precision. Exact verification prevents false positives.

The binary kernel-sampling argument is correct. The solver sees only A^T x, so conditional on that right side, taking the coefficient at basis element 1 preserves uniformity on the binary kernel. Verification and retries do not introduce dependence on hidden roots.

Consequently Proposition 10.15 retains its claimed final complexity and congruence after the local corrections.


Section 11: CORRECT.

The smooth-part identity is exact. Product/remainder trees, modular squaring and gcd computations give the required soft-linear batch cost.

The search recognises precisely the filtered count, subject to factor exits. Trial division of the two selected smooth values costs soft-O(B), already included.

There is no remaining HECM dependency in this search.


Section 12: CORRECT.

With fixed theta<1,
c(a)=max(2a, a+1/sqrt(2), a+theta/(2a))
is minimised at a=theta/sqrt(2).

At this choice, shape testing and box searches both cost L^(1/sqrt(2)+o(1)); multiplication by the row count gives (1+theta)/sqrt(2). Linear algebra has exponent sqrt(2)theta, strictly smaller because theta<1.

The L^o(1) loss from field selection is permissible for fixed k. It does not change the leading constant.


Section 13: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

Complete factorisation, certified primality, perfect powers and nonsquarefree inputs are handled correctly in substance.

Trial division removes the small-factor obstruction. Recomputing parameters for each cofactor is legitimate. The recursion has O(log N) calls.

Add structural guards to make the dovetailed branch a total algorithm, and replace the envelope sentence by the epsilon argument in issue 13.


Section 14: CORRECT as a limited experimental report.

The disclosed deviations prevent these experiments from testing the analytic relation-supply theorems or the asymptotic constant. The manuscript largely makes that limitation clear.

The reported runs were not independently reproduced in this audit. They support the implemented algebra but cannot validate the first or second moment.


Section 15: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

The limitations concerning fixed parameters, impractical constants and the unproved recursive variant are appropriate. Correct the interpretation of the speculative 0.851 value.

The random-integer square-dependence threshold is not a theorem prohibiting this architecture. The cited sharp-threshold result concerns independent uniformly sampled integers. [Balister–Bollobás–Morris](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2018/188-1/p02)


Appendix A: CORRECT WITH FIXES.

Apart from the overbroad formulation of the imported fact, Parts 1 and 2 work in the actual regime.

In Part 1, only the principal ell component is removed. Smooth integers are coprime to ell, so the remaining character sum is exactly the sum for the s component. The use of W, rather than the smaller max-residue sum, is essential. The range Q/ell=N^(q_0-2nu+o(1)) has a positive power lower bound and lies below sqrt(Psi).

Its large-conductor error, relative to Psi times the family density, has exponent
q_0-2nu-xi/2<0.

In Part 2, a nonprincipal character modulo the prime ell has conductor ell. Thus every character considered has conductor exceeding Y. With
eta=min(1/100,nu/xi),
one has eta<1/80 and x^eta<Y with a fixed exponent margin. Harper’s Proposition 2 therefore applies after enlargement by positivity. [Harper, Proposition 2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5992)

The two relative errors have exponents
q_0-xi/2<0
and -eta xi<0.
The family density is N^(-o(1)), so it cannot erase these margins.

Finally, u/log^2 u is of order sqrt(log N)/(log log N)^(5/2), which dominates log log N. The required absorption of fixed logarithmic losses is valid here.

A self-contained rewrite would improve the paper, but importing an explicitly bounded intermediate quantity from a published proof is mathematically legitimate. I do not identify the known TODO as a fatal gap.

WHOLE-PAPER VERDICT: ACCEPT WITH MINOR FIXES
WEAKEST LINK: Appendix A’s character-majorant import; its precise range and exceptional-character bookkeeping should be stated explicitly before publication.
